            "Java",
            "Variables",
            "Beginner"
          ]
        },
        "chapter": "M1: Java Fundamentals"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "narration": "The word primitive means basic or fundamental. These are the simplest building blocks built directly into Java. They are like pre-made containers that are ready to use the moment you start coding.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "ArchitectureFlow",
        "props": {
          "title": "What is a Primitive?",
          "activeStepIndex": 0,
          "steps": [
            {
              "title": "Primitive",
              "desc": "Built-in, basic building blocks"
            },
            {
              "title": "Non-Primitive",
              "desc": "Complex objects like Strings"
            }
          ]
        },
        "keyIdea": "Primitives are basic building blocks",
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "containers",
            "text": "Pre-defined by Java",
            "kind": "note"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "narration": "Why do we need different types? Because computers have limited memory. You would not use a massive truck to deliver a single pencil. Using the right type saves space and makes your program run faster.",
      "remotion": {
